(window.FREE_Q = window.FREE_Q || {})["xxiv"] = {"qs": [["SEBI Guidelines on AML/CFT/PF Standards", "A client's trading suddenly shifts from small trades in large caps to heavy daily trading in one illiquid small-cap, matched with the same counterparties. The intermediary should:", ["Ignore it, since trading volumes are the client's personal choice", "Inform the client that an STR is being considered", "Increase the client's credit limit to support more trades", "Review the activity and consider filing an STR"], 3, "Abrupt changes and matched trades in illiquid scrips can indicate manipulation or laundering. The firm should examine the activity and report if suspicious, without tipping off the client."], ["Introduction to AML, CFT and PF", "A client buys and sells the same illiquid shares repeatedly across several related accounts to obscure the source of funds. This activity mainly represents which stage of money laundering?", ["Placement of cash into the banking system for the first time", "Integration of clean-looking funds into the legitimate economy", "Layering", "Structuring of cash deposits below reporting thresholds"], 2, "Layering uses complex, often circular transactions to separate funds from their criminal origin. Placement introduces illicit cash into the system, and integration brings laundered money back as apparently legitimate wealth."], ["SEBI Guidelines on AML/CFT/PF Standards", "SEBI's 'Clients of Special Category' include:", ["All salaried resident individuals below 30 years of age", "Every client who uses a mobile trading app", "Clients who hold only mutual fund units", "Non-resident clients and high-net-worth clients"], 3, "CSCs include NRIs, HNIs, trusts and charities, PEPs, companies with close family shareholding, clients from high-risk jurisdictions and others needing more scrutiny."], ["AML, CFT and PF Guidelines", "Targeted financial sanctions require an intermediary that identifies a designated person's account to:", ["Allow the person to withdraw all funds before freezing", "Freeze the funds without delay and report to the authorities", "Inform the client first and seek permission to freeze", "Wait until the next annual audit to take any action"], 1, "Under UN sanctions and India's UAPA and WMD Act orders, funds of designated persons must be frozen immediately without prior notice, and the authorities informed as prescribed."], ["Prevention of Money Laundering Act, 2002", "A reporting entity fails to report suspicious transactions as required. Under the PMLA, the Director, FIU-IND may:", ["Imprison the reporting entity's clients for seven years", "Impose a monetary penalty for each failure", "Cancel the reporting entity's SEBI registration directly", "Order the closure of the stock exchange"], 1, "The Director, FIU-IND can impose fines on reporting entities and their officers for each failure to comply with reporting and record-keeping obligations. Registration is a matter for SEBI."], ["Introduction to AML, CFT and PF", "FIU-IND functions under the administrative control of the:", ["Ministry of Home Affairs (Internal Security Division)", "Ministry of Finance (Department of Revenue)", "Reserve Bank of India's Department of Supervision", "Ministry of External Affairs"], 1, "FIU-IND is the central national agency under the Department of Revenue, Ministry of Finance, reporting to the Economic Intelligence Council. It receives, analyses and disseminates financial intelligence."], ["PML (Maintenance of Records) Rules, 2005", "Reports furnished to FIU-IND by a reporting entity must be kept confidential because:", ["FIU-IND charges a fee for every report that is shared with clients", "Disclosing them to the client could tip off suspects", "The reports contain the entity's annual profit and loss accounts", "SEBI publishes them on its website after one month"], 1, "The PML Rules require reporting entities to keep reports confidential. Tipping off a client could allow suspects to move funds or destroy evidence and defeat the investigation."], ["Introduction to AML, CFT and PF", "The Egmont Group is best described as:", ["A network of national Financial Intelligence Units", "A United Nations committee that freezes terrorist assets worldwide", "An association of stock exchanges that sets listing standards", "A global body of central banks that sets capital rules for banks"], 0, "The Egmont Group connects FIUs across countries to share financial intelligence securely. FIU-IND is a member. Asset freezing lists come from the UN Security Council, and capital rules from the Basel Committee."], ["SEBI Guidelines on KYC Norms", "Video-based customer identification (V-CIP) for securities market KYC is acceptable if:", ["The client sends a pre-recorded video by e-mail", "It is live, recorded, secure and done by trained staff", "The client is identified only through a voice call", "A friend of the client confirms the identity on video"], 1, "SEBI permits video-based IPV and KYC where the interaction is live, recorded, geotagged and conducted with security safeguards by authorised officials."], ["Prevention of Money Laundering Act, 2002", "Under the PMLA, the term 'reporting entity' includes:", ["Only public sector banks regulated by the RBI", "Only listed companies with a market capitalisation above ₹1,000 crore and their promoters and directors", "Banks, financial institutions, intermediaries and designated businesses", "Only individuals with income above ₹50 lakh a year"], 2, "Reporting entities include banking companies, financial institutions, intermediaries such as brokers and depository participants, and persons carrying on designated businesses or professions."], ["PML (Maintenance of Records) Rules, 2005", "A Non-Profit Organisation Transaction Report (NTR) under the PML Rules covers receipts by an NPO above:", ["₹50,000 in a single day only", "₹1 crore in a financial year", "₹2 lakh in any cash transaction", "₹10 lakh or its equivalent in foreign currency"], 3, "Reporting entities must report all transactions involving receipts by non-profit organisations of value more than ₹10 lakh or its equivalent in foreign currency."], ["Scheduled Offences", "A broker's client generates large profits through insider trading and then moves them into property. The PMLA is relevant because:", ["Property purchases are always illegal under the PMLA when made out of trading profits in shares", "Insider trading is only a civil matter with no criminal angle", "Insider trading is a scheduled offence and the profits are proceeds of crime", "The PMLA applies only to cash deposits in banks"], 2, "Proceeds derived from a scheduled offence are proceeds of crime, and dealing with them — such as projecting them as untainted property — can amount to money laundering."], ["Scheduled Offences", "Which of these offences is included in the PMLA Schedule?", ["Breach of a rental agreement between landlord and tenant", "Failure to file a GST return on time for one month", "Offences relating to narcotic drugs under the NDPS Act", "Non-payment of a credit card bill by an individual"], 2, "Drug trafficking offences under the NDPS Act are classic predicate offences for money laundering. Civil defaults and minor procedural lapses are not scheduled offences."], ["Prevention of Money Laundering Act, 2002", "Bail for an accused under the PMLA is subject to 'twin conditions', which require the court to be satisfied that:", ["There are reasonable grounds to believe the accused is not guilty and is unlikely to offend on bail", "The accused has paid all income tax dues for the last ten years and has never been arrested before", "The accused has surrendered his passport to the stock exchange", "The scheduled offence has been completely tried and decided"], 0, "Section 45 imposes twin conditions for bail after giving the public prosecutor an opportunity to oppose. The Supreme Court upheld these conditions in Vijay Madanlal Choudhary (2022)."], ["Discussion on PMLA Cases", "In Pankaj Bansal v. Union of India (2023), the Supreme Court held that at the time of arrest under the PMLA, the ED must:", ["Obtain written consent of the accused person before the arrest", "Publish the arrest on its website before informing the person", "Wait until the scheduled offence trial is completely over", "Furnish the grounds of arrest to the arrested person in writing"], 3, "The Court held that grounds of arrest must be supplied in writing to give effect to the constitutional right to be informed, enabling the arrested person to seek bail or legal remedy."], ["AML, CFT and PF Guidelines", "Under India's framework, freezing of assets of persons linked to WMD proliferation is implemented through an order under:", ["Section 2 of the Indian Contract Act, 1872", "Section 80C of the Income-tax Act", "The SEBI (Mutual Funds) Regulations", "Section 12A of the WMD Act, 2005"], 3, "The 2022 amendment to the Weapons of Mass Destruction Act introduced Section 12A, empowering the government to freeze funds of persons involved in proliferation financing."], ["SEBI Guidelines on KYC Norms", "For a company client, KYC requires identification of:", ["Beneficial owners and authorised signatories", "Only the company's statutory auditor", "Only the company's largest supplier", "Only its registered office landlord"], 0, "Intermediaries must verify the company's constitution, identify beneficial owners who own or control it, and verify persons authorised to act on its behalf."], ["Scheduled Offences", "Offences under the Prevention of Corruption Act, 1988 are included in the PMLA Schedule. This means that:", ["Public servants can never be investigated under any law", "Corruption is no longer an offence under other laws", "Only the CBI can investigate any PMLA case", "Bribe money can be pursued through money laundering proceedings"], 3, "Since corruption offences are scheduled, the bribe amount and assets derived from it can be attached and prosecuted under the PMLA, in addition to proceedings under the PC Act."], ["SEBI Guidelines on AML/CFT/PF Standards", "An intermediary must ensure its foreign branches and subsidiaries follow AML standards that are:", ["Only those of the host country, whatever they are", "Optional, as decided by the local branch head", "The stricter of Indian and host-country requirements", "Lower than Indian standards to reduce costs"], 2, "SEBI requires that, where requirements differ, branches and subsidiaries abroad apply the higher standard, to the extent the host country's laws permit."], ["AML, CFT and PF Guidelines", "A 'risk-based approach' allows an intermediary to:", ["Ignore all low-value clients for AML purposes completely and focus only on institutional clients", "Apply the same checks to every client regardless of risk", "Apply simplified measures to lower-risk clients and stronger measures to higher-risk ones", "Avoid reporting suspicious transactions of low-risk clients"], 2, "Resources are focused where risks are higher, with simplified CDD only where risk is demonstrably low. Reporting obligations apply to all clients regardless of risk rating."], ["Scheduled Offences", "Part B of the PMLA Schedule mainly covers:", ["All offences under the Motor Vehicles Act", "Disputes between shareholders and companies over dividends, bonus issues and rights entitlements", "Civil cases for recovery of debts", "Offences under the Customs Act involving a value above a threshold"], 3, "Part B covers offences under the Customs Act involving a total value of ₹1 crore or more (the specified threshold), such as false declarations. Part A covers a wide list of offences with no value threshold."], ["Prevention of Money Laundering Act, 2002", "Appeals against orders of the Adjudicating Authority under the PMLA lie to:", ["The Appellate Tribunal", "The Securities Appellate Tribunal in all cases", "The FIU-IND Director directly", "The Income-tax Appellate Tribunal"], 0, "The PMLA provides for an Appellate Tribunal to hear appeals against Adjudicating Authority orders, with a further appeal to the High Court on questions of law."], ["Prevention of Money Laundering Act, 2002", "A provisional attachment order under the PMLA must be confirmed by which authority to continue?", ["The stock exchange where the shares are listed", "The Adjudicating Authority", "The Principal Officer of the reporting entity", "The Registrar of Companies"], 1, "After the Enforcement Directorate provisionally attaches property, it files a complaint before the Adjudicating Authority, which decides whether to confirm the attachment."], ["PML (Maintenance of Records) Rules, 2005", "For an unincorporated association or body of individuals, the beneficial ownership threshold under the PML Rules is ownership or entitlement of more than:", ["10% of property, capital or profits", "25% of property, capital or profits", "15% of property, capital or profits", "51% of property, capital or profits"], 2, "For unincorporated associations or bodies of individuals, the threshold is more than 15%. Companies and partnership firms have a 10% threshold after the 2023 amendments."], ["AML, CFT and PF Guidelines", "Which client characteristic is most likely to require enhanced due diligence?", ["A salaried employee investing through a monthly SIP", "A pensioner holding a small portfolio of blue-chip shares", "Complex ownership through layers of offshore companies", "A student opening an account with complete KYC and a small balance"], 2, "Opaque structures can hide beneficial owners and are a classic risk indicator. Simple, transparent profiles with small or regular transactions are generally lower risk."], ["Scheduled Offences", "Fraud under the Companies Act, 2013 (Section 447) being a scheduled offence means that:", ["All companies must register with FIU-IND as NPOs", "Proceeds of corporate fraud can be targeted under PMLA", "Directors are exempt from PMLA proceedings", "The Companies Act no longer applies to fraud"], 1, "Corporate fraud under Section 447 is a scheduled offence, so assets derived from it can be attached and laundering prosecuted, alongside action by the SFIO or other agencies."], ["Introduction to AML, CFT and PF", "Trade-based money laundering typically involves:", ["Mis-invoicing goods to move value across borders", "Buying units of liquid mutual funds and holding them for years", "Paying income tax in advance through challans", "Opening a demat account with complete KYC"], 0, "Over- or under-invoicing imports and exports lets criminals transfer value disguised as trade. Securities intermediaries should note trade-linked companies whose flows do not fit their profile."], ["Discussion on PMLA Cases", "A broker files an STR on a client who later is not charged with any offence. The broker:", ["Must pay compensation to the client in every case", "Loses its registration automatically", "Must disclose the STR to the client afterwards", "Is protected if the STR was made in good faith"], 3, "The PMLA protects reporting entities and their officers from civil or criminal proceedings for reports made in good faith. Confidentiality of the STR continues."], ["AML, CFT and PF Guidelines", "Under the FATF Recommendations, enhanced due diligence for a foreign politically exposed person typically includes:", ["Opening the account without any documents to save time", "Reducing monitoring of the relationship, because PEPs are well-known and trusted public figures", "Senior management approval and establishing the source of wealth and funds", "Refusing all transactions above ₹1,000 permanently"], 2, "PEPs carry higher corruption risk. Firms must obtain senior management approval, establish source of wealth and funds, and conduct enhanced ongoing monitoring of the relationship."], ["SEBI Guidelines on KYC Norms", "Which client is exempted from the PAN requirement for transacting in the securities market, subject to verification?", ["Any client investing less than ₹10 crore", "All NRIs, irrespective of the amount invested", "All clients aged above 60 years, irrespective of residence or the amount they invest", "Investors residing in Sikkim, subject to residence verification"], 3, "SEBI allows limited exemptions, such as investors residing in Sikkim, government officials in an official capacity and certain others, subject to verification of identity and residence."], ["Scheduled Offences", "A person commits an offence abroad which, if committed in India, would be a scheduled offence, and the proceeds are moved into Indian shares. How does the PMLA treat this?", ["The PMLA never applies to any offence committed outside India", "Only the foreign country can take action, and Indian assets cannot be attached", "It can be a scheduled offence with cross-border implications", "It is treated only as a tax matter under the Income-tax Act"], 2, "The PMLA Schedule includes offences with cross-border implications, so proceeds of such crimes brought into India can be attached and laundering prosecuted here, often with international cooperation."], ["Discussion on PMLA Cases", "A compliance lesson from PMLA case law for reporting entities is that:", ["Good records and timely STRs support investigations", "Records can be destroyed once the client relationship ends", "Reporting suspicious transactions is optional if the client is valuable", "Tipping off clients is acceptable after filing an STR"], 0, "Investigations rely heavily on reporting entities' records and reports. Firms must retain records for the prescribed period and never tip off clients."], ["Introduction to AML, CFT and PF", "Why is terrorist financing sometimes harder to detect than money laundering?", ["Terrorist financing always involves very large cash deposits", "Terrorist funds are always routed through stock exchanges", "Terrorist financing is not a crime under Indian law", "Funds may be small and come from legitimate sources"], 3, "TF can use modest amounts from legitimate income or donations, so the warning signs are subtle. The focus is on where money is going, while ML focuses on where it came from."], ["AML, CFT and PF Guidelines", "An intermediary should screen its client base against sanctions lists:", ["At onboarding and whenever lists are updated", "Only once, at the time of account opening", "Only when the client asks for a statement", "Only if the client is a foreign national"], 0, "Sanctions lists change frequently. Screening at onboarding and on every list update ensures newly designated persons are identified and their assets frozen without delay."], ["Discussion on PMLA Cases", "In Vijay Madanlal Choudhary, the Court held that the twin conditions for bail under Section 45 are:", ["Applicable only to offences under the NDPS Act", "Reporting suspicious transactions is optional if the client is valuable and has a long relationship", "Constitutional and reasonable given the gravity of the offence", "Replaced by ordinary bail provisions of the CrPC"], 2, "The Court upheld the twin conditions, finding them reasonable for the serious offence of money laundering, after Parliament's 2018 amendment addressed earlier defects."], ["SEBI Guidelines on KYC Norms", "The Central KYC Records Registry (CKYCR) helps the financial sector by:", ["Providing a common KYC repository across regulators", "Rating clients' creditworthiness for loans", "Approving IPO allotments for investors", "Fixing brokerage rates for trades"], 0, "CKYCR, operated by CERSAI, holds KYC records that banks, insurers and securities intermediaries can access, reducing repeated KYC and improving consistency."], ["Introduction to AML, CFT and PF", "A country placed by FATF under 'increased monitoring' is commonly said to be on the:", ["Black list with a call for counter-measures", "Grey list", "White list of fully compliant jurisdictions", "Watch list maintained by FIU-IND for clients"], 1, "Jurisdictions under increased monitoring (grey list) have strategic AML/CFT deficiencies but are working with FATF on an action plan. The 'call for action' list is the more severe black list."], ["SEBI Guidelines on KYC Norms", "Under SEBI's KRA framework, a KYC record that has been verified with the issuing source, such as the Income Tax database or Aadhaar, is marked as:", ["Rejected", "Validated", "On hold", "Registered only"], 1, "SEBI requires KRAs to verify key attributes like PAN, name, address and mobile/e-mail with official sources. Records verified in this way are marked 'validated' and can be used across intermediaries."], ["PML (Maintenance of Records) Rules, 2005", "When an existing client's KYC documents become outdated, the reporting entity must:", ["Continue using the old records indefinitely", "Update the client's identification records periodically", "Close the account without informing the client", "Ask the stock exchange to update the records"], 1, "Client due diligence is an ongoing obligation. Reporting entities must periodically update KYC information, more frequently for high-risk clients, and when there is reason to doubt records."], ["PML (Maintenance of Records) Rules, 2005", "A reporting entity forms a suspicion about a client on a Monday. Its suspicious transaction report must be furnished to FIU-IND:", ["Only at the end of the financial year with other reports", "Within one hour in every case, before the trading session closes on the day of the suspicion", "Only if the client agrees to the report being filed", "Within seven working days of being satisfied that the transaction is suspicious"], 3, "The PML Rules require an STR within seven working days of the Principal Officer concluding that a transaction is suspicious. Client consent is never sought, and tipping off is prohibited."], ["Scheduled Offences", "Which of the following is an example of a scheduled offence relevant to securities markets?", ["Late filing of an income tax return by a salaried person", "Failure to update a mobile number with the depository", "Fraudulent and unfair trade practices under the SEBI Act", "Delay in paying a mobile phone bill"], 2, "Offences under the SEBI Act, such as manipulative and deceptive practices or insider trading, are scheduled offences. Proceeds from them can therefore be the subject of money laundering cases."], ["Discussion on PMLA Cases", "Market manipulation cases involving synchronised trades in illiquid shares to create bogus long-term gains show that:", ["Long-term capital gains are always genuine", "Illiquid stocks cannot be used for laundering", "Brokers have no role in identifying such trades, as surveillance is entirely the exchange's job", "Exchange alerts and broker monitoring are vital red-flag sources"], 3, "Schemes that ramp up penny stocks to generate fake gains were detected through exchange surveillance and broker alerts. Intermediaries must review such patterns and report STRs."], ["Prevention of Money Laundering Act, 2002", "Statements recorded by an ED officer under Section 50 of the PMLA are significant because:", ["They can only be recorded in the presence of the accused person's lawyer and a magistrate", "They are made to the police and so are never admissible in any proceedings", "The officer can summon persons and record statements that are admissible as evidence", "They can be recorded only after the person has been convicted of the scheduled offence"], 2, "Section 50 gives ED officers civil-court-like powers to summon persons and documents. Persons must state the truth, and such statements can be used as evidence."], ["AML, CFT and PF Guidelines", "Correspondent relationships and 'nested' accounts raise AML concern because:", ["They always involve cash deposits at bank branches", "They are prohibited under all Indian laws", "They reduce transaction volumes in the market", "Underlying customers of the respondent may not be known"], 3, "When a foreign institution uses an account to serve its own clients, the intermediary may not see the ultimate customers. FATF requires extra checks on respondent institutions' controls."], ["Discussion on PMLA Cases", "A key lesson from cases involving shell companies used for accommodation entries is that intermediaries should:", ["Accept any company that has a valid PAN and incorporation certificate", "Look beyond paperwork to the company's real activity and owners", "Treat all newly incorporated companies as low risk", "Avoid checking directors, since that is the RoC's job"], 1, "Shell entities often have valid documents but no real business. Checking business substance, beneficial owners and fund flows helps detect accommodation entries and laundering."], ["SEBI Guidelines on KYC Norms", "If a client's KYC record at the KRA is marked 'on hold' because the e-mail or mobile number could not be verified, the intermediary should:", ["Get the details corrected before allowing transactions", "Allow unlimited trading until the next annual review", "Close the account and transfer the securities to itself", "Ignore the status since it is not important"], 0, "An 'on hold' status signals incomplete or unverified details. Clients cannot transact until the missing or incorrect information is corrected and re-verified."], ["SEBI Guidelines on KYC Norms", "A client's KYC documents show a different name from his PAN. The intermediary should:", ["Open the account using whichever name the client prefers", "Resolve the mismatch before opening the account", "Open the account but block only withdrawals", "Ignore minor differences in name in all cases"], 1, "KYC requires consistent identity information. Name mismatches must be resolved with supporting documents before onboarding, to avoid fictitious or benami accounts."], ["Prevention of Money Laundering Act, 2002", "Property confiscated to the Central Government under the PMLA may be restored to a claimant with a legitimate interest if:", ["The Special Court is satisfied the claimant suffered loss and acted in good faith", "The claimant files a request with the stock exchange where the confiscated shares are listed", "The claimant pays a fee to FIU-IND", "The accused agrees to the restoration in writing"], 0, "The PMLA allows the Special Court to order restoration of confiscated property to persons with a legitimate interest who suffered a quantifiable loss, such as victims or bona fide lenders."], ["SEBI Guidelines on AML/CFT/PF Standards", "Hiring procedures under SEBI's AML guidelines require intermediaries to:", ["Hire only relatives of existing directors", "Screen employees for integrity and suitability", "Avoid any background checks to protect privacy", "Let clients choose which employees handle their accounts"], 1, "Adequate screening when hiring, especially for sensitive roles, reduces insider risk. Staff with access to client data and transactions must be trustworthy."], ["Prevention of Money Laundering Act, 2002", "Under the PMLA, records of transactions must be maintained by a reporting entity for at least:", ["One year from the date of the last transaction", "Three months after the account is closed", "Five years from the date of transaction", "Permanently, without any time limit"], 2, "The PMLA requires records of transactions to be kept for five years from the date of transaction, and identity records for five years after the business relationship ends."], ["Introduction to AML, CFT and PF", "Which agency is responsible for investigating money laundering offences and attaching proceeds of crime under the PMLA?", ["The Financial Intelligence Unit – India, which analyses reports", "The Securities and Exchange Board of India", "The Central Board of Direct Taxes", "The Directorate of Enforcement"], 3, "The Directorate of Enforcement investigates PMLA offences, attaches property and prosecutes. FIU-IND receives and analyses reports, SEBI regulates intermediaries and CBDT administers direct taxes."], ["PML (Maintenance of Records) Rules, 2005", "A cross-border wire transfer report must be filed by applicable reporting entities for transfers above:", ["₹50,000, only for transfers within India", "₹1 crore, only if paid in cash", "₹5 lakh, where the source or destination is outside India", "₹10,000, for any transfer by any person, whether within India or to a foreign country"], 2, "The PML Rules require reporting of cross-border wire transfers of more than ₹5 lakh or equivalent where either the origin or destination of the funds is in India and the other end abroad."], ["SEBI Guidelines on AML/CFT/PF Standards", "Under SEBI's AML master circular, an intermediary's client acceptance policy should ensure that:", ["No account is opened in a fictitious or benami name", "Accounts are opened even if clients refuse to give KYC documents", "Clients with sanctioned names can trade below a certain value", "Accounts are opened only for clients introduced by employees"], 0, "SEBI requires that accounts are not opened in anonymous, fictitious or benami names and that clients are identified and verified before the relationship begins."], ["Discussion on PMLA Cases", "In Vijay Madanlal Choudhary (2022), the Supreme Court held that money laundering under Section 3:", ["Applies only if the person is caught depositing cash at a bank", "Covers any process or activity connected with proceeds of crime", "Requires all of placement, layering and integration to be proved", "Does not cover concealment or possession of proceeds"], 1, "The Court read Section 3 broadly: concealment, possession, acquisition, use or projecting proceeds as untainted are each independent activities that can constitute the offence."], ["SEBI Guidelines on KYC Norms", "For an FPI client, KYC requirements are generally:", ["Waived completely because FPIs are foreign", "Risk-based, with categories determining documentation", "The same as for a domestic retail investor", "Limited to collecting a photograph only"], 1, "SEBI applies a risk-based KYC approach for FPIs, with documentation varying by FPI category and beneficial owners identified as required under the PML Rules."], ["Introduction to AML, CFT and PF", "Proliferation financing risk for a securities intermediary arises mainly when:", ["A client may be linked to WMD programmes or sanctioned persons", "A client invests regularly through a monthly SIP in equity schemes", "A client changes his registered mobile number and e-mail address", "A client asks for a consolidated account statement once a year"], 0, "PF involves financing weapons of mass destruction and their delivery systems, often via front companies tied to sanctioned states or persons. Routine investing or KYC updates are not indicators."], ["Discussion on PMLA Cases", "Following Vijay Madanlal Choudhary, the provisional attachment of property under Section 5 is:", ["Unconstitutional and no longer permitted", "Allowed only after the accused's conviction", "Valid, with safeguards such as recorded reasons", "Possible only with consent of the property owner"], 2, "The Court upheld Section 5, noting built-in safeguards such as recording reasons in writing, a time limit and confirmation by the Adjudicating Authority."], ["PML (Maintenance of Records) Rules, 2005", "The Designated Director of a reporting entity under the PML Rules is:", ["An external auditor appointed by FIU-IND to inspect the reporting entity's AML systems every year", "The client's nominee who receives securities on death", "The officer of the stock exchange who supervises brokers", "A director nominated to ensure overall compliance with PMLA obligations"], 3, "Each reporting entity must designate a director — such as the managing director or a whole-time director — responsible for ensuring overall PMLA compliance. FIU-IND must be informed of the designation."], ["SEBI Guidelines on AML/CFT/PF Standards", "An intermediary should conduct training on AML for:", ["Front-line, compliance and back-office staff", "Only the board of directors once in five years", "Only staff who have been with the firm for over ten years", "Only external auditors"], 0, "SEBI expects ongoing employee training so staff understand AML policies, red flags and reporting procedures. Front-line staff are often the first to notice unusual activity."], ["SEBI Guidelines on AML/CFT/PF Standards", "When an intermediary relies on a KYC Registration Agency's records for a client, it should:", ["Treat the client as low risk without any further review", "Still perform its own risk assessment and ongoing monitoring", "Delete its own copy of the client's documents, since the KRA already maintains the official record", "Stop all transaction monitoring for that client"], 1, "KRA records help with identification, but the intermediary remains responsible for its risk categorisation, ongoing due diligence and reporting obligations."], ["SEBI Guidelines on AML/CFT/PF Standards", "The Principal Officer under SEBI's AML framework should be:", ["A junior trainee working only on client calls", "An external consultant with no access to records", "The client's chartered accountant", "A sufficiently senior officer able to act independently"], 3, "SEBI expects the Principal Officer to be senior enough to discharge duties independently, with access to all records, and to report to senior management or the board."], ["Scheduled Offences", "Under the Supreme Court's interpretation, the PMLA offence depends on the scheduled offence in that:", ["Proceeds of crime must arise from criminal activity relating to a scheduled offence", "The ED may proceed even if no scheduled offence is alleged anywhere", "The scheduled offence must first be tried by the ED itself and result in conviction before any PMLA action", "Any civil breach of contract is sufficient"], 0, "Money laundering is linked to proceeds generated from a scheduled offence. If there is no scheduled offence or it is finally quashed or ends in acquittal, there can be no proceeds of crime."], ["PML (Maintenance of Records) Rules, 2005", "When a client is a partnership firm, the PML Rules treat as beneficial owner a natural person who holds more than what share of capital or profits?", ["10%", "25%", "15%", "50%"], 0, "Following the 2023 amendments, the threshold for partnership firms is ownership or entitlement to more than 10% of capital or profits, or control through other means."], ["AML, CFT and PF Guidelines", "FATF's recommendations on new technologies require firms to:", ["Assess ML/TF risks before launching new products or technologies", "Avoid using any digital onboarding method at all", "Allow anonymous accounts for online investors", "Stop all KYC once a client uses a mobile app"], 0, "Before launching new products, delivery channels or technologies, firms should identify and mitigate the related risks. Digital onboarding is allowed with appropriate controls."]]};
